Yes, here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Find the index of the second-to-last space (the first adjective)
  const firstAdjIndex = words.lastIndexOf(" ", words.length - 2);

  // Swap the positions of the first and second adjectives
  [words[firstAdjIndex], words[firstAdjIndex + 1]] = [words[firstAdjIndex + 1], words[firstAdjIndex]];

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  return words.join(" ");
}

// Example usage:
const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park.";
const modifiedSentence = switchAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(modifiedSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park."
```

The `switchAdjectives()` function takes a sentence as its argument, splits it into an array of words, finds the index of the second-to-last space (which should be the index of the first adjective), swaps the positions of the first and second adjectives, and then joins the words back into a sentence. The modified sentence is returned by the function.

This function assumes that there will always be at least two adjectives in the sentence, but it should still work correctly even if there are more than two. If there is only one adjective or no adjectives at all, the function will simply return the original sentence unchanged.

