To make the circle element look more 3D, you can apply some CSS properties such as perspective, rotateX, and transform-style. Here's how you can modify the existing CSS for the circle element:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray);
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
  perspective: 800px;
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
}

.circle:hover {
  transform: translateZ(10px) rotateX(-10deg);
}
```

Let's go through each property:

1. `perspective`: This property sets up the 3D space in which the object is rendered. In this case, we set the value to 800px, which determines the distance between the viewer and the imaginary vanishing point on the z-axis (the center of the circle). The larger the value, the farther away the vanishing point is, making objects appear smaller and flatter.

2. `transform-style`: This property specifies whether or not child elements inherit the transform origin. We set this property to `preserve-3d` so that any nested elements inside the circle will also be part of the 3D scene.

3. `transform`: This property is used to animate the transformation of the circle when hovering over it. In this example, we use the `translateZ()` function to move the circle along the z-axis by 10 pixels, and `rotateX()` to tilt it slightly (-10 degrees in this case).

By applying these properties, you can make your circle look more 3D and add depth to it. You may need to adjust the values of `perspective` and `transform` to achieve the desired effect.

