Here's a possible implementation of the requested function:

```javascript
function findMatchesInOptions(options, dotNotationString) {
  const keys = dotNotationString.split('.');
  let currentObj = options;

  for (let I = 0; I < keys.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!currentObj[keys[i]]) {
      return []; // Key not found at this level, stop searching
    }

    currentObj = currentObj[keys[i]];
  }

  const lastKey = keys[keys.length - 1];
  const matches = {};
  for (let j = 0; j < currentObj.length; j++) {
    if (currentObj[j][lastKey] === dotNotationString.slice(dotNotationString.indexOf('.') + 1)) {
      matches[j] = currentObj[j];
    }
  }

  return Object.values(matches);
}
```

This function takes two arguments: `options`, which is the array of objects containing the options to search through, and `dotNotationString`, which is the key path in dot notation that we want to match against within the options. The function returns an array of objects from `options` whose key with the given path contains the given value. If no such object is found, an empty array is returned instead.

The function works by splitting the dot notation string into an array of keys, then recursively navigating the hierarchy of `options` using those keys until it reaches the end of the path. If any key along the way cannot be found, the function stops searching and returns an empty array immediately. Once the end of the path has been reached, the function iterates over the remaining options at that level, checking whether their key with the given name matches the remaining part of the dot notation string. Matched objects are stored in a dictionary `matches`, which is later converted to an array and returned.

Note that this implementation assumes that there can only be one key-value pair per object with the given name, as specified by the original requirement. If multiple such pairs exist, the function will only return the first one encountered. If you need to modify this behavior, you may need to change the matching logic accordingly.

